description:
## Summary

`FragmentLibrary.refresh()` calls `indexBody()`, which awaits a vault read and then writes that
read's result into the shared `Fragment` object, `bodyIndex`, and `preview` / `searchText`. Neither
`refresh` nor `indexBody` is guarded by the per-path write queue or by any sequence number, and
nothing re-checks freshness after the `await`.

Because a single user edit makes `src/main.ts` call `refresh(file)` **twice** (`vault.on("modify")`
and `metadataCache.on("changed")`, plus the internal `refresh` at the end of `saveBody`), several
`indexBody` calls for the same path overlap. A read issued after an earlier write can resolve *after*
a read issued after a later write, and the older snapshot wins permanently.

The result is a stale `preview` and a stale `searchText` — the body search index — that nothing
repairs, because `indexBody` is the only writer of both.

## Root cause

`refresh` is not queued; only `saveBody` / `saveScratchpad` / `updateProperties` use `queue()`:

```ts
// src/library.ts
async refresh(file: TFile): Promise<void> {
  if (!this.contains(file.path)) return;
  this.updateMetadata(file);
  await this.indexBody(file);      // <-- not serialized, no sequence guard
  this.emit();
}

private async indexBody(file: TFile): Promise<void> {
  try {
    const body = bodyOf(await this.app.vault.cachedRead(file));   // <-- T0 read
    const item = this.fragments.get(file.path);
    if (!item) return;
    this.bodyIndex.set(file.path, body);        // <-- lands whenever the read resolves
    item.preview = plainPreview(body);
    item.searchText = `${item.title} ${item.collection} ${item.tags.join(" ")} ${body}`.toLowerCase();
  } catch {
    // A file may disappear while the background index is reading it.
  }
}
```

`indexBody` re-reads `this.fragments.get(file.path)` *after* the await, so it always mutates
whatever the current entry is — the entry has no notion of which body version it represents. There is
no `active` flag, no generation check, and no post-read comparison against the file's current
content. The `catch` also swallows read failures, so a failed read silently leaves whatever the
previous snapshot was.

Concretely, in `src/main.ts` a single edit fans out to overlapping refreshes:

```ts
this.registerEvent(this.app.vault.on("modify", (file) => { if (file instanceof TFile) void this.library.refresh(file); }));
...
this.registerEvent(this.app.metadataCache.on("changed", (file) => { void this.library.refresh(file); }));
```

and `saveBody` / `updateProperties` / `create` each call `await this.refresh(file)` themselves.

## Impact

- The list row keeps showing the **old** preview text for a fragment the user just edited.
- More seriously, `searchText` is the body portion of the search index. The README advertises
  "search across titles, tags, collections, and note bodies" and the list search runs with
  `includeBody: true`. A stale `searchText` makes body search silently miss the newest text (and can
  still match text that is no longer in the file) until the file is modified again.
- The state is sticky: nothing re-indexes it at session end, so the wrong index persists for the
  lifetime of the view.
- Rapid typing is the highest-risk path, because each autosave writes and then refreshes, so
  successive refreshes overlap by construction.

## Reproduction (deterministic)

Driven against the **real** `FragmentLibrary` with an in-memory vault. The only test double controls
the *completion order* of `cachedRead`; each read snapshots file content at call time exactly as a
real read does. This models a legal async-I/O reordering and assumes nothing about library
behaviour.

Setup: `Fragments/Alpha.md` with body `ORIGINAL`; `await lib.load()`; then hold the first
`cachedRead` so it completes last.

Rapid typing — autosave #1 writes `EDITED-1` then refreshes, autosave #2 writes `EDITED-2` then
refreshes. Each refresh issues its read *after* its own write, as the code does:

```
after load                  preview = "ORIGINAL"

file.content = "EDITED-1"; const first  = lib.refresh(file);   // read issued after write #1
file.content = "EDITED-2"; const second = await lib.refresh(file); // read issued after write #2

after refresh #2 (newest)   preview = "EDITED-2"     // correct
// --- the older read #1 finally resolves; nothing guards it ---
after refresh #1 (older)    preview = "EDITED-1"     // STALE, and it is now permanent

STALE PREVIEW : true
STALE SEARCH  : true
```

Expected: after all in-flight reads settle, `preview` and `searchText` reflect the newest body
(`EDITED-2`, the content on disk).
Actual: the older snapshot (`EDITED-1`) wins and stays.

### Control

With a single `refresh` per edit, or with reads that complete in issue order, the values are correct —
the defect requires only an out-of-order completion, which ordinary concurrent I/O produces. The
library has no ordering guarantee, so correctness currently depends on vault read timing.

## Suggested fix

Give `indexBody` a per-path sequence guard, and drop the result if a newer read has already been
applied. A minimal version:

```ts
private readonly reads = new Map<string, number>();

private async indexBody(file: TFile): Promise<void> {
  const path = file.path;
  const seq = (this.reads.get(path) ?? 0) + 1;
  this.reads.set(path, seq);
  try {
    const body = bodyOf(await this.app.vault.cachedRead(file));
    if (this.reads.get(path) !== seq) return;   // a newer read already won
    const item = this.fragments.get(path);
    if (!item) return;
    this.bodyIndex.set(path, body);
    item.preview = plainPreview(body);
    item.searchText = `${item.title} ${item.collection} ${item.tags.join(" ")} ${body}`.toLowerCase();
  } catch {
    // A file may disappear while the background index is reading it.
  }
}
```

Alternatively, route `refresh` through the existing per-path `queue()` so refreshes for one path are
serialized, and delete stale entries from `reads` in `remove()`. Either fix must also keep the
`load()` generation check working, since `load()`'s batch loop issues the same unsequenced reads.

## Steps to Reproduce

1. Seed `Fragments/Alpha.md`.
2. Load the library, then edit the file twice in quick succession, so two `refresh` calls overlap
   (this is what `vault.on("modify")` + `metadataCache.on("changed")` + the internal `saveBody`
   refresh produce on every keystroke).
3. Let all reads settle.
4. Compare the indexed `preview` / `searchText` with the file content on disk, and search the list
   for text from the newest edit.

Expected: the index reflects the newest body; body search finds the newest text.
Actual: the older snapshot is retained, and body search misses the newest text.

## Acceptance Criteria

- After all in-flight reads for a path settle, `preview`, `bodyIndex` and `searchText` reflect the
  newest body regardless of read completion order.
- Body search finds text from the most recent edit.
- A regression test forces out-of-order read completion for one path and asserts the newest body
  wins.

`useKeyboard` in `src/app.tsx` registers its keydown handler on the **whole Obsidian document** instead of the Fragments view, so Ctrl/Cmd+K, Ctrl/Cmd+S and Escape are swallowed for every pane in that window while the view is mounted.

## Mechanism

```ts
// src/app.tsx, useKeyboard
const doc = view.containerEl.ownerDocument;
const onKey = (event: KeyboardEvent): void => {
  if ((event.ctrlKey || event.metaKey) && event.key.toLowerCase() === "k") {
    event.preventDefault();
    setDialog("palette");
  }
  if ((event.ctrlKey || event.metaKey) && event.key.toLowerCase() === "s") {
    event.preventDefault();
    if (!(event.target instanceof HTMLElement && event.target.closest(".fragments-scratchpad")))
      void save();
  }
  if (event.key === "Escape") setDialog(null);
};
doc.addEventListener("keydown", onKey);
return () => doc.removeEventListener("keydown", onKey);
```

`view.containerEl.ownerDocument` is the `document` of the entire Obsidian window (the popout window opened by `prepareLeaf` in `src/main.ts`, or the main window). `keydown` bubbles from any element to the document, so the handler runs for keystrokes aimed at the note editor, Obsidian's own inputs, the sidebar, and any other plugin's UI. The handler never checks that the event originated inside `view.containerEl`, that the Fragments view is the active view, or that `document.activeElement` belongs to Fragments.

The handler stays mounted for the whole lifetime of the view (`useController` calls `useKeyboard(view, save, setDialog)` unconditionally), and `src/main.ts` reopens the view on `onLayoutReady` whenever a Fragments leaf is restored, so this is the normal state, not an edge case.

## Impact

- `Ctrl/Cmd+K` and `Ctrl/Cmd+S` are `preventDefault()`-ed window-wide while Fragments is open, so those chords are dead for the note editor and for any other plugin that binds them in that window.
- Pressing Ctrl+S in an unrelated note pane silently saves the *current fragment* instead (or, when nothing is selected/dirty, still swallows the key).
- Escape anywhere in the window closes the Fragments dialog.

The plugin already has `addCommand` entries in `src/main.ts` (Open / New fragment / Search fragments), which is Obsidian's own scoped-hotkey mechanism and is the natural home for these two chords.

## Reproduction

Minimal DOM shim (a node tree with bubbling dispatch) with the registration body above copied verbatim. The Fragments `viewContainer` and an unrelated note editor are both children of `#app`, as they are in Obsidian:

```js
doc.addEventListener("keydown", onKey);           // doc = viewContainer.ownerDocument
noteEditor.dispatch("keydown", { key: "k", ctrlKey: true, target: noteEditor,
                                 preventDefault: () => { defaultPrevented = true; } });
```

Output:

```
inside view.containerEl?       false
preventDefault called?         true
Fragments dialog opened?       true
```

The key event comes from a node outside the view container and still triggers `preventDefault()` and opens the Fragments palette.

## Suggested fix

Scope the handler to the view, e.g. early-return unless the event target is inside the view container:

```ts
if (event.target instanceof Node && !view.containerEl.contains(event.target)) return;
```

or move Ctrl/Cmd+K and Ctrl/Cmd+S into `addCommand({ hotkeys: [...] })` in `src/main.ts`, which Obsidian only dispatches for the active view.

## Summary

The command palette (`Ctrl+K`) lists snippets from **all** fragments, but selecting one routes
through `choose()`, which does **not** reset the active search scope. `useController` then has an
auto-select effect that immediately overrides any selection that is not present in the current
`filtered` list. So whenever a collection, tag, Starred, or Recent filter is active, picking a
snippet from the palette silently opens a **different** fragment than the one the user chose.

The palette is the one selection surface that crosses the filter boundary, and it is the only one
that forgot to clear the filter — `chooseGlobal` (the header search) does exactly that.

## Affected code

`src/app.tsx` — `useController` (`paletteItems`, `choosePalette`, `choose`, `chooseGlobal`) and the
auto-select effect.

`paletteItems` is built from `entries` (the whole library), ignoring `scope` — by design, the palette
is a global surface:

```ts
// src/app.tsx:512
const paletteItems = entries
  .filter((item) => `${item.title} ${item.tags.join(" ")}`.toLowerCase().includes(paletteQuery.toLowerCase()))
  .sort((a, b) => b.modified - a.modified)
  .slice(0, 8);
```

`choosePalette` then selects through `choose`, which only sets the path:

```ts
// src/app.tsx:445
const choose = (path: string): void => {
  setSelected(path);
  setMobilePane("editor");
};

// src/app.tsx:518
const choosePalette = (index: number): void => {
  const item = paletteItems[index];
  if (item) {
    choose(item.path);      // <-- no scope reset
    setDialog(null);
    return;
  }
  if (index === paletteItems.length) void createFragment();
};
```

Contrast `chooseGlobal`, used by the header search, which deliberately clears the filter — this is
what the README promises ("finds titles and tags across the entire library, even while a collection
or tag is selected"):

```ts
// src/app.tsx:449
const chooseGlobal = (path: string): void => {
  setScope({ kind: "all" });
  setTagFilter("");
  setQuery("");
  choose(path);
};
```

The auto-select effect then discards the out-of-view selection:

```ts
// src/app.tsx:400-403
useEffect(() => {
  if (selected && filtered.some((item) => item.path === selected)) return;
  setSelected(filtered[0]?.path ?? null);
}, [selected, entries, filtered]);
```

## Impact

- The user's explicit choice is silently discarded and a different fragment opens with no error.
- `choose` also switches the mobile pane to `"editor"`, so the wrong fragment is what the user then
  sees on a phone.
- If the active filter yields no matches at all (e.g. the Starred scope and the user picked an
  unstarred snippet), `filtered` is empty, the selection becomes `null`, and the editor is replaced
  by the "Your fragment library" welcome screen — the user believes the snippet vanished.
- Any palette entry outside the current collection is affected, which is the normal case: the palette
  lists the whole library, so most entries are outside a narrow filter.

## Reproduction (deterministic)

Vault: `Fragments/Work/Alpha.md`, `Fragments/Work/Beta.md`, `Fragments/Home/Gamma.md`.

1. Click the **Work** collection in the sidebar. `scope = { kind: "collection", value: "Work" }`.
2. Press `Ctrl+K`, type `gamma`, press `Enter`.

Observed, using the real `FragmentLibrary` and the real `searchFragments`, with the two
`useController` expressions copied verbatim from `src/app.tsx`:

```
scope          : {"kind":"collection","value":"Work"}
filtered (list): [ 'Alpha', 'Beta' ]
palette shows  : [ 'Gamma @ Fragments/Home/Gamma.md' ]

user picked    : Fragments/Home/Gamma.md
selected after : Fragments/Work/Alpha.md
expected       : Fragments/Home/Gamma.md
MISMATCH       : true
```

The palette offered `Gamma`; after Enter the selection is `Alpha`.

### Control

With `scope = { kind: "all" }`, `filtered` contains every fragment, so `filtered.some(...)` is true
for `Gamma` and the selection sticks. The override is therefore caused by the active scope, not by
the palette itself.

## Suggested fix

Route palette selection through the same scope-clearing path as the header search:

```tsx
const choosePalette = (index: number): void => {
  const item = paletteItems[index];
  if (item) {
    chooseGlobal(item.path);   // clears scope, tagFilter and query, like the header search
    setDialog(null);
    return;
  }
  if (index === paletteItems.length) void createFragment();
};
```

Alternatively, make the auto-select effect skip overriding when the selection was set
intentionally, but clearing the scope matches the README's stated behaviour for cross-filter
surfaces.

## Steps to Reproduce

1. Seed three fragments: `Fragments/Work/Alpha.md`, `Fragments/Work/Beta.md`,
   `Fragments/Home/Gamma.md`.
2. Open Fragments and click the **Work** collection in the sidebar.
3. Press `Ctrl+K`, type `gamma`, press `Enter`.
4. Observe which fragment the editor is showing.

Expected: the `Gamma` snippet opens, and the scope is cleared as it is for the header search.
Actual: the editor shows `Alpha` (the first fragment in the `Work` filter).

## Acceptance Criteria

- Choosing a snippet from the command palette opens exactly that snippet, regardless of the active
  collection/tag/starred/recent scope or the list search query.
- The scope-clearing behaviour matches the header search (`chooseGlobal`).
- When the active filter is empty, selecting a palette entry still opens the chosen snippet instead
  of the welcome screen.
- A regression test covers: scope=collection + palette pick outside that collection.

## Summary

`Preview` in `src/app.tsx` renders Markdown with `void MarkdownRenderer.render(...)`
and keeps no stale-guard. `MarkdownRenderer.render` is asynchronous, and `Preview` has
no `key`, so React reuses the same component instance and the *same* `ref` `<div>` when
the selected fragment changes. A render started for the previous fragment is never
cancelled and still writes into that div, so the preview pane ends up showing content
from a fragment the user is no longer looking at.

## Root cause

```tsx
function Preview({ body, path, view, plugin, language }) {
  const ref = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    const element = ref.current;
    if (!element) return;
    element.empty();
    if (findLanguage(language)?.id === "markdown") {
      void MarkdownRenderer.render(plugin.app, body, element, path, view);  // <-- no guard
      return;
    }
    const code = element.createEl("pre").createEl("code");
    highlightCode(code, body, language);
  }, [body, path, view, plugin, language]);
  return <div className="fragments-preview markdown-rendered" ref={ref} />;
}
```

`element.empty()` only clears what has rendered *so far*; it cannot cancel a render that
is still in flight. Every effect re-run for a new `body`/`path` therefore races the
previous render against the shared `element`.

This is inconsistent with the rest of the file: `useDraft` guards its async read with
the standard `let active = true` + cleanup flag. `Preview` has no equivalent.

Note the effect depends on `body` and `path` independently, so a single fragment switch
runs the effect more than once, which is why the stale content accumulates instead of
being replaced once.

## Impact

The preview pane can display another fragment's rendered Markdown while the title,
toolbar and editor all show the selected fragment. Garbled or duplicated content also
accumulates across switches. Any Markdown renderer that awaits (Obsidian's does — it
awaits post-processing and component rendering) is affected, so the window is real
rather than theoretical, and it is widest for large notes and notes with embeds.

## Reproduction

Verified by mounting the real `AppShell` in jsdom with a stubbed `MarkdownRenderer`
whose per-body delay is controlled, and both fragments set to `language: markdown`
(fragment A delayed 250 ms, fragment B 10 ms):

1. With `Alpha` selected, switch the editor to Preview mode. A's render is in flight.
2. Before it resolves, click the `Beta` row.
3. Wait for both renders to settle.

Observed:

```
selected: Beta | preview: "BBB-MARKDOWNAAA-MARKDOWNAAA-MARKDOWN"
expected: "BBB-MARKDOWN"
```

The pane shows `BBB` (the selected fragment) followed by two copies of `AAA` from the
superseded renders.

## Suggested fix

Add the same stale-guard `useDraft` already uses, and key the container on the source
path so a fragment switch cannot reuse the previous render target:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const element = ref.current;
  if (!element) return;
  let active = true;
  element.empty();
  if (findLanguage(language)?.id === "markdown") {
    void MarkdownRenderer.render(plugin.app, body, element, path, view).then(() => {
      if (!active) element.empty();
    });
    return () => { active = false; };
  }
  const code = element.createEl("pre").createEl("code");
  highlightCode(code, body, language);
  return () => { active = false; };
}, [body, path, view, plugin, language]);
```

Optionally render `<div key={path} ... />` so React discards the old subtree outright.

Trashing a fragment with unsaved edits discards the draft and then shows a spurious "Fragment is no longer available." error, because `trash` is the only path-mutating action that does not flush the draft first.

## Mechanism

In `useController` (`src/app.tsx`) the path-mutating actions are inconsistent. `rename` (line 487) and `move` (line 496) flush the draft before touching the vault:

```ts
const rename = async (value: string): Promise<void> => {
  ...
  await save(false);
  setSelected(await library.rename(selectedItem.path, value));
};
```

`trash` (line 502) does not:

```ts
const trash = async (): Promise<void> => {
  if (!selectedItem || !window.confirm(...)) return;
  try {
    await library.trash(selectedItem.path);
    setSelected(null);
  } catch (error) {
    errorNotice(error as Error);
  }
};
```

`useDraft` keeps the in-flight draft in a ref and flushes it from an effect cleanup keyed on `selected`:

```ts
const pendingDraft = useRef<{ path: string; text: string } | null>(null);
...
useEffect(
  () => () => {
    const writing = pendingDraft.current;
    if (writing?.path === selected)
      void library.saveBody(writing.path, writing.text, savedBody.current).catch(errorNotice);
  },
  [selected, library],
);
```

`trash()` calls `setSelected(null)`, so `selected` changes and that cleanup runs. `pendingDraft.current` still points at the fragment that was just trashed (`trash` never clears it, and `dirty` is still true), so it calls `saveBody` for a path that no longer exists. `FragmentLibrary.file()` throws for it:

```ts
private file(path: string): TFile {
  const file = this.app.vault.getAbstractFileByPath(path);
  if (!(file instanceof TFile) || !this.contains(file.path))
    throw new Error("Fragment is no longer available.");
  return file;
}
```

The rejection is caught by `.catch(errorNotice)`, so the user gets a red Notice for a fragment they deliberately deleted, and the un-saved text is never written to the trashed file.

## Impact

- Unsaved edits are silently lost on trash (the trashed file keeps the last *saved* body), unlike rename and move, which persist the draft first.
- A misleading "Fragment is no longer available." Notice is raised for the user's own delete action.
- `dirty` stays `true` in `useDraft` after the selection is cleared.

## Trigger

Type in a fragment and hit trash within the 700 ms autosave window, or after the autosave failed (a conflict leaves `dirty` true indefinitely).

## Reproduction

The real `src/library.ts` bundled with esbuild (`obsidian` aliased to a stub of `getFrontMatterInfo` / `normalizePath` / `TFile` / an in-memory vault), then executing the `useDraft` cleanup body verbatim for the trashed path:

```js
const path = await library.create();
const pendingDraft = { path, text: "unsaved edits the user would lose" };
const selected = path;                                  // state at trash() time
await library.trash(path);                              // app.tsx trash()
// setSelected(null) -> effect deps [selected, library] changed -> cleanup:
if (pendingDraft?.path === selected)
  await library.saveBody(pendingDraft.path, pendingDraft.text, "");
```

Output:

```
created: Fragments/Untitled fragment.md
RESULT: flush REJECTED -> Fragment is no longer available.
```

The same `file()` guard that throws here is what makes the failure deterministic: the trashed path is gone from the vault tree, so the flush can never succeed.

## Relationship to fragments-nzo

`fragments-nzo` also reports missing state hygiene in `useDraft`, but with a different trigger and a different fix: it covers the `!selected` load branch (line 114) that wipes `body`/`draft` without resetting `dirty`/`loadedPath`, triggered by a no-match search on a fragment whose path still exists. This issue is about `trash` omitting the draft flush for a path that no longer exists, and the two proposed fixes are in different functions. Neither issue's fix resolves the other: resetting `dirty` when the selection is nulled would silence the spurious Notice but still leave the un-saved text out of the trashed file.

## Suggested fix

Flush the draft like the sibling actions do, so the trashed file carries the user's latest text and no post-delete flush is attempted:

```ts
const trash = async (): Promise<void> => {
  if (!selectedItem || !window.confirm(`Move “${selectedItem.title}” to trash?`)) return;
  try {
    await save(false);
    await library.trash(selectedItem.path);
    setSelected(null);
  } catch (error) {
    errorNotice(error as Error);
  }
};
```

`save(false)` rethrows on a conflict, which already surfaces through `errorNotice` and would abort the trash. Optionally also clear `pendingDraft`/`dirty` when `selected` becomes null in `useDraft`, so a later flush can never target a removed path.

## Summary

`useDraft`'s load effect decides whether to overwrite the editor buffer from a `dirty` value that is **captured when the effect starts**. `dirty` is not a dependency of that effect, so a read that is already in flight can never see edits the user makes while it is pending. When it resolves it unconditionally runs:

```ts
setBody(text); setDraft(text); setDirty(false); pendingDraft.current = null;
```

The result is the worst possible failure mode for an editor: **the text the user just typed is silently discarded, and the footer flips to "Saved"** — a false assurance that the text reached disk. The fragment is left in a clean, fully-"saved" state, so the autosave, the Save button and Ctrl+S all become no-ops for the lost keystrokes. The user keeps typing into an editor that is no longer accumulating a draft, and closes the view believing their work is stored.

## Affected code

`src/app.tsx` — `useDraft`, the fragment-load effect:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
  if (!selected) { setBody(""); setDraft(""); return undefined; }
  let active = true;
  void library.read(selected).then((text) => {
    if (active && (!dirty || loadedPath.current !== selected)) {
      loadedPath.current = selected;
      savedBody.current = text;
      setBody(text);
      setDraft(text);
      setDirty(false);
      pendingDraft.current = null;     // <-- discards the user's in-flight draft
    }
  }).catch(errorNotice);
  return () => { active = false; };
}, [selected, modified, library]);      // <-- `dirty` is NOT a dependency
```

`active` only guards against the effect being **torn down**; there is no equivalent guard for the user having **typed** since the read was issued. `edit()` has no way to invalidate the in-flight read:

```ts
const edit = (path: string, text: string): void => {
  pendingDraft.current = { path, text };
  setDraft(text);
  setDirty(true);
};
```

The load effect re-runs on every `modified` change, and `modified` is refreshed constantly — Obsidian Sync, a second window, or the plugin's own `refresh()` after a write all bump it. So the window opens frequently; it only needs a `library.read` that is not instantaneous (cold cache, a large note, a busy or remote vault).

## Trigger

1. A fragment is open and clean, so `dirty === false` and the editor shows the on-disk text.
2. The file is modified externally (or by the plugin's own refresh). `modified` changes, so the load effect re-issues `library.read(selected)` while `dirty` is still `false`.
3. Before that read resolves, the user types. `edit()` sets `pendingDraft` and `dirty = true` — but the in-flight `.then` closure still holds `dirty === false`, and there is no check.
4. The read resolves with the pre-keystroke text. The guard evaluates `(!false || ...)` → `true`, so the buffer is replaced and `setDirty(false)` is called.

## Impact

- **Silent data loss with a false "Saved" indicator.** This is strictly worse than showing an unsaved draft: the user is actively told the text is persisted. Nothing prompts them to suspect a problem, and the content is gone.
- Recovery is manual and non-obvious — the user must notice the text reverted and retype it.
- The README advertises "Edits also save after a short pause" and that Fragments "reads changes made to the Markdown files outside the app". The re-read behaviour is correct; discarding an edit made during it is not.

## Reproduction

Mounted the real `AppShell` in jsdom against an in-memory vault. `library.read` is delayed 200 ms to model a slow vault read; nothing else is stubbed. The re-read is triggered exactly as an external edit would, by bumping `modified` and emitting while the fragment is clean.

```
initial editor: "Body of Alpha\n"
t+30ms  editor: "USER TYPED THIS" | footer: 15 chars · 3 wordsUnsaved changesSave changes
t+430ms editor: "Body of Alpha\n" | footer: 14 chars · 3 wordsLast edited 20721d agoSaved
t+1.6s  editor: "Body of Alpha\n" | footer: 14 chars · 3 wordsLast edited 20721d agoSaved
